From 1dc2df1ac028bc8c97547331699eb9eab5c06671 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 18:19:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: after a failure, try native output again with a
 later video

After a sustained failure (Dolby Vision frames that could not be shown
for two seconds, or a native setup refused), native Dolby Vision output
was disabled until Kodi restarted. The most likely cause of such a
failure at home is the TV itself: switched off or to another input
during a film. Kodi also read the EDID only once, at start: a TV input
setting that changes what the TV advertises (an LG input set up for
games advertises only low-latency Dolby Vision) needed a Kodi restart.

- Native output disabled by a failure is tried again with a later
  video: the next one after a first failure, then the one after 2, 4 and
  at most 8 videos that opened (a source that fails to open does not
  count). A minute of playback shown natively (pauses excluded) resets
  that wait.
- When a video starts (once per file, before its input and streams
  open), the display is looked at again: once a display that offers
  Dolby Vision has been seen in this session, or after a failure, so
  nothing changes for other displays. The connector is probed
  (nvidia-drm and i915 read the display's state and EDID again; amdgpu
  reports what its hotplug handling found) and the EDID contents are
  compared (a property blob id can be reused).
- A changed EDID replaces the display information of the Dolby Vision
  decisions. The HDR decisions keep the one of Kodi's start, as in
  Kodi. No EDID, or one that cannot be read (a TV waking up), blocks
  native output for this video only (reason "edid") and changes nothing
  else. When the
  display is back or what native output depends on changed (not, for
  example, the audio formats of a sound bar behind the TV), the wait is
  reset and native output is tried with this video. A display found
  disconnected gets the ordinary output (reason "output").
- None of this happens in the middle of a video: whether native output
  is possible changes only when a video starts, before its decoder is
  set up for native output or not, or with a failure (towards the
  ordinary output). A stream opened without native output never
  switches to it, so the enhancement layer and the metadata checks are
  always set up for what is shown.
- The Dolby Vision intent of the render manager is evaluated again for
  the first video stream of each file: the next file of a playlist
  reuses the player without closing its video stream, and kept the
  previous file's intent. The refresh rate is still chosen as in Kodi.
- Every change of the retry state is made under one lock.
- The player information shows the new reason text.

Idea from the HDMI recovery of the Intel Dolby Vision fork (R0.2.1a-opt1:
no permanent exclusion, reset on a connector or EDID change), implemented
independently: the fork retries every second during playback, which with
a signal change at every attempt would mean repeated resynchronizations
of the TV.
